1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:09,548
1975. Inside a Kodak laboratory in Rochester,
New York, an engineer named Steven

2
00:00:09,588 --> 00:00:12,378
Sasson was building
something extraordinary.

3
00:00:12,876 --> 00:00:16,064
It weighed about eight pounds. It
captured black-and-white images.

4
00:00:16,104 --> 00:00:19,154
And it recorded them onto magnetic tape.

5
00:00:19,656 --> 00:00:22,725
The resolution? Just ten thousand pixels.

6
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:25,866
By modern standards, it was primitive.

7
00:00:26,455 --> 00:00:28,312
But the principle was revolutionary.

8
00:00:28,948 --> 00:00:32,531
A camera that could take photographs
without photographic film.

9
00:00:32,983 --> 00:00:35,326
And the company behind it was Kodak.

10
00:00:36,999 --> 00:00:42,706
Thirty-seven years later, on January
nineteenth, 2012, Eastman Kodak filed for

11
00:00:42,746 --> 00:00:45,016
Chapter Eleven bankruptcy protection.

12
00:00:45,547 --> 00:00:49,668
The company that helped pioneer digital
photography had failed to build a

13
00:00:49,708 --> 00:00:52,718
sustainable business through
the industry's transformation.

14
00:00:53,112 --> 00:00:55,280
The popular explanation is simple.

15
00:00:55,786 --> 00:00:59,794
Kodak invented the digital camera,
ignored it, and paid the price.

16
00:01:00,311 --> 00:01:02,046
But that isn't quite what happened.

17
00:01:02,477 --> 00:01:05,428
Kodak didn't simply ignore
digital photography.

18
00:01:06,058 --> 00:01:07,485
It invested in it.

19
00:01:08,046 --> 00:01:10,772
Which makes the real question
far more interesting.

20
00:01:11,368 --> 00:01:15,292
How did Kodak see the future and
still lose its position in it?

21
00:01:16,890 --> 00:01:21,174
For much of the twentieth century, Kodak
was one of the most recognizable names in

22
00:01:21,214 --> 00:01:22,214
photography.

23
00:01:22,484 --> 00:01:27,943
Its products helped millions of people
capture birthdays, weddings, holidays and

24
00:01:27,983 --> 00:01:29,113
ordinary moments.

25
00:01:29,679 --> 00:01:34,346
The company made cameras, photographic
film, paper and processing equipment.

26
00:01:35,025 --> 00:01:38,833
And its famous yellow packaging
became part of everyday life.

27
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:42,877
But Kodak's success wasn't
simply about making cameras.

28
00:01:43,432 --> 00:01:48,955
Its real strength was the enormous commercial
system surrounding every photograph.

29
00:01:49,482 --> 00:01:52,464
A system that encouraged
customers to keep buying.

30
00:01:54,144 --> 00:01:56,773
Consider the economics of
traditional photography.

31
00:01:57,445 --> 00:01:59,243
A customer bought a camera.

32
00:01:59,903 --> 00:02:01,354
Then they bought film.

33
00:02:01,835 --> 00:02:06,489
After taking photographs, they paid to
have that film developed and printed.

34
00:02:07,017 --> 00:02:08,620
Then they bought more film.

35
00:02:09,070 --> 00:02:14,985
For Kodak, photography created recurring
demand for products and services long after

36
00:02:15,025 --> 00:02:16,695
the original camera purchase.

37
00:02:17,203 --> 00:02:20,814
This was an extraordinarily
attractive business structure.

38
00:02:21,245 --> 00:02:23,356
But it depended on something fundamental.

39
00:02:23,941 --> 00:02:26,483
People had to keep using physical film.

40
00:02:26,947 --> 00:02:31,491
And digital photography threatened
to remove that requirement entirely.

41
00:02:33,329 --> 00:02:37,551
Sasson's prototype demonstrated that
a photograph could become electronic

42
00:02:37,591 --> 00:02:39,301
information.

43
00:02:39,359 --> 00:02:40,913
No chemical development.

44
00:02:41,586 --> 00:02:43,623
No conventional film negative.

45
00:02:44,211 --> 00:02:47,261
No physical print required
to see the image.

46
00:02:47,724 --> 00:02:50,953
But the technology was far
from ready for mass adoption.

47
00:02:51,536 --> 00:02:56,703
The camera was bulky, image quality was
limited, and storing photographs was slow.

48
00:02:57,170 --> 00:02:58,840
Kodak had time to respond.

49
00:02:59,453 --> 00:03:03,947
And over the following decades, the company
continued developing digital imaging

50
00:03:03,987 --> 00:03:04,987
technology.

51
00:03:05,549 --> 00:03:09,134
The challenge wasn't simply recognizing
what might become possible.

52
00:03:09,757 --> 00:03:14,400
It was understanding what would happen to
Kodak's business when that possibility

53
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:15,870
became normal.

54
00:03:17,521 --> 00:03:22,297
Imagine running a company whose most valuable
business depends on customers buying

55
00:03:22,337 --> 00:03:23,607
something repeatedly.

56
00:03:24,242 --> 00:03:29,299
Now imagine developing a technology that
allows those customers to stop buying it.

57
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:32,001
That was Kodak's strategic dilemma.

58
00:03:32,352 --> 00:03:35,426
Digital photography offered
enormous possibilities.

59
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:41,190
But it also threatened the recurring
economics of film, processing and printing.

60
00:03:41,773 --> 00:03:46,173
The company needed to develop a new business
while its established business remained

61
00:03:46,213 --> 00:03:47,283
valuable.

62
00:03:47,662 --> 00:03:51,861
And the new technology didn't automatically
offer the same profitability.

63
00:03:52,633 --> 00:03:56,809
Kodak had to manage two very
different economic worlds at once.

64
00:03:58,623 --> 00:04:01,740
Here's the part of the story
that often gets overlooked.

65
00:04:02,387 --> 00:04:04,840
Kodak did not abandon digital photography.

66
00:04:05,474 --> 00:04:10,591
In 1991, it introduced a
professional digital camera system.

67
00:04:11,023 --> 00:04:16,496
In 1995, it launched the DC40
consumer digital camera.

68
00:04:16,915 --> 00:04:22,469
And in 2001, it introduced the EasyShare
system, designed to make digital

69
00:04:22,509 --> 00:04:26,019
photography easier for ordinary customers.

70
00:04:26,532 --> 00:04:30,454
These were real products, backed
by engineering and investment.

71
00:04:31,023 --> 00:04:35,169
So the story isn't that Kodak
refused to enter the digital market.

72
00:04:35,674 --> 00:04:40,719
The story is that entering a market and
building a profitable position within it are

73
00:04:40,759 --> 00:04:42,809
two very different achievements.

74
00:04:44,731 --> 00:04:50,213
Kodak understood that consumers wanted
simpler ways to take, store, share and print

75
00:04:50,253 --> 00:04:51,253
photographs.

76
00:04:51,652 --> 00:04:57,332
Its EasyShare cameras and docking systems
were designed around that experience.

77
00:04:57,901 --> 00:05:02,220
The company also invested in digital
printing and imaging technologies.

78
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:06,123
But digital cameras were becoming
increasingly competitive.

79
00:05:06,833 --> 00:05:08,864
Hardware prices faced pressure.

80
00:05:09,345 --> 00:05:11,197
Technology improved quickly.

81
00:05:11,687 --> 00:05:13,646
And customers gained more choices.

82
00:05:14,262 --> 00:05:19,105
Kodak could participate in the transition
without controlling the economics of the

83
00:05:19,145 --> 00:05:20,145
new industry.

84
00:05:20,468 --> 00:05:25,362
The company that had dominated the film
ecosystem was now competing in a very

85
00:05:25,402 --> 00:05:26,532
different market.

86
00:05:28,178 --> 00:05:31,146
Digital photography changed
more than the camera.

87
00:05:31,557 --> 00:05:34,840
It changed what happened after
someone pressed the shutter.

88
00:05:35,304 --> 00:05:40,088
Photographs could be stored on computers,
shared electronically and viewed without

89
00:05:40,128 --> 00:05:41,128
printing.

90
00:05:41,310 --> 00:05:46,498
That weakened the relationship between taking
pictures and buying film, chemicals or

91
00:05:46,538 --> 00:05:47,460
paper.

92
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:52,586
And as digital imaging spread, the traditional
photographic supply chain came under

93
00:05:52,626 --> 00:05:53,626
pressure.

94
00:05:53,746 --> 00:05:58,079
Kodak's challenge was not simply to
sell a different kind of camera.

95
00:05:58,119 --> 00:06:03,139
It was to replace an entire economic
system that had supported the company for

96
00:06:03,179 --> 00:06:04,179
generations.

97
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:11,008
This is where technological leadership
and business success begin to separate.

98
00:06:11,539 --> 00:06:16,258
A company can invent important technology
and still struggle to earn attractive

99
00:06:16,298 --> 00:06:17,692
returns from it.

100
00:06:17,732 --> 00:06:21,904
Kodak had expertise, patents and
a globally recognized brand.

101
00:06:22,358 --> 00:06:27,059
But digital imaging demanded different
capabilities, different investments and

102
00:06:27,099 --> 00:06:28,789
different sources of profit.

103
00:06:29,210 --> 00:06:31,979
Meanwhile, the old film
business was declining.

104
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:37,794
The transition required Kodak to fund
new opportunities while managing the

105
00:06:37,834 --> 00:06:40,084
deterioration of established ones.

106
00:06:40,616 --> 00:06:45,099
And eventually, the financial
pressure became impossible to ignore.

107
00:06:46,931 --> 00:06:52,283
By 2011, Kodak's financial statements
showed a company under severe strain.

108
00:06:52,696 --> 00:06:57,403
Annual revenue had fallen to approximately
six billion dollars, down from roughly

109
00:06:57,443 --> 00:07:00,433
seven point two billion the previous year.

110
00:07:00,824 --> 00:07:03,606
The company reported a
substantial net loss.

111
00:07:04,203 --> 00:07:09,211
Its filings described declining traditional-product
volumes, competitive pressure in

112
00:07:09,251 --> 00:07:12,961
digital cameras and rising material costs.

113
00:07:13,538 --> 00:07:15,442
This wasn't one product failing.

114
00:07:15,849 --> 00:07:21,044
It was a business trying to finance a
transformation while its historical sources of

115
00:07:21,084 --> 00:07:22,614
revenue weakened.

116
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:26,626
The numbers revealed how difficult
that transformation had become.

117
00:07:28,306 --> 00:07:34,313
On January nineteenth, 2012, Kodak and
certain United States subsidiaries filed for

118
00:07:34,353 --> 00:07:36,403
Chapter Eleven bankruptcy protection.

119
00:07:36,897 --> 00:07:39,839
The filing was not the
disappearance of the company.

120
00:07:40,269 --> 00:07:42,495
It was a legal restructuring process.

121
00:07:43,064 --> 00:07:48,023
Kodak continued operating while attempting
to reorganize its obligations and focus

122
00:07:48,063 --> 00:07:49,553
its remaining businesses.

123
00:07:50,186 --> 00:07:52,560
But the symbolic impact was enormous.

124
00:07:53,220 --> 00:07:58,088
A name that had once represented the future
of photography was now restructuring in

125
00:07:58,128 --> 00:07:59,858
the middle of the digital age.

126
00:08:00,446 --> 00:08:03,779
The company had helped create
a technological revolution.

127
00:08:04,421 --> 00:08:08,514
It had not successfully transformed
its original business around it.

128
00:08:10,094 --> 00:08:14,884
During restructuring, Kodak made difficult
decisions about what would remain.

129
00:08:15,463 --> 00:08:19,029
It moved away from much of its
consumer digital-camera business.

130
00:08:19,604 --> 00:08:24,550
It sold assets associated with online
photo services and other operations.

131
00:08:25,297 --> 00:08:28,514
It also reached agreements to
sell digital imaging patents.

132
00:08:29,373 --> 00:08:34,214
The company increasingly concentrated on
commercial printing, packaging and imaging

133
00:08:34,254 --> 00:08:35,254
technologies.

134
00:08:35,817 --> 00:08:40,622
These decisions reflected a different
future from the consumer photography empire

135
00:08:40,662 --> 00:08:42,212
Kodak had once built.

136
00:08:42,631 --> 00:08:44,606
The brand was still recognizable.

137
00:08:45,419 --> 00:08:47,485
But the company behind it was changing.

138
00:08:49,232 --> 00:08:56,077
On September third, 2013, Kodak emerged from
Chapter Eleven as a reorganized company

139
00:08:56,117 --> 00:08:58,987
focused primarily on business imaging.

140
00:08:59,591 --> 00:09:01,107
Kodak had not vanished.

141
00:09:01,773 --> 00:09:06,418
It continued operating in commercial
printing, film and related technologies.

142
00:09:07,067 --> 00:09:12,200
Some consumer imaging activities and brand
relationships continued through separate

143
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:14,710
businesses and licensing arrangements.

144
00:09:15,364 --> 00:09:20,414
The important distinction is that Kodak's
bankruptcy did not mean photography itself

145
00:09:20,454 --> 00:09:21,504
had disappeared.

146
00:09:22,112 --> 00:09:25,556
People were taking more
digital photographs than ever.

147
00:09:25,928 --> 00:09:30,421
What had changed was the economic structure
of the industry, and Kodak's position

148
00:09:30,461 --> 00:09:31,461
within it.

149
00:09:32,908 --> 00:09:37,879
So was Kodak's mistake inventing a
technology that threatened its own business?

150
00:09:38,552 --> 00:09:39,509
No.

151
00:09:39,549 --> 00:09:41,672
Innovation wasn't the mistake.

152
00:09:42,153 --> 00:09:46,815
The deeper failure was the inability to
build a sufficiently strong and sustainable

153
00:09:46,855 --> 00:09:49,625
business as the market
moved away from film.

154
00:09:50,132 --> 00:09:52,599
Kodak recognized digital imaging.

155
00:09:53,261 --> 00:09:54,482
It developed products.

156
00:09:55,188 --> 00:09:57,134
It invested in technology.

157
00:09:57,730 --> 00:10:02,743
But its historic advantages didn't translate
automatically into the new industry.

158
00:10:03,444 --> 00:10:05,323
That is the danger of disruption.

159
00:10:05,935 --> 00:10:11,045
A company can understand where technology
is going without successfully changing how

160
00:10:11,085 --> 00:10:12,085
it makes money.

161
00:10:13,660 --> 00:10:18,070
Kodak's story isn't simply about a
company that ignored the future.

162
00:10:18,490 --> 00:10:23,238
It's about a company that helped create
the future, participated in it, and still

163
00:10:23,278 --> 00:10:26,028
struggled to survive the
economic transition.

164
00:10:26,574 --> 00:10:29,863
Its old business model was
extraordinarily successful.

165
00:10:30,501 --> 00:10:34,070
Its new businesses couldn't replace
that success quickly enough.

166
00:10:34,615 --> 00:10:36,517
And that is the final lesson.

167
00:10:36,984 --> 00:10:41,495
Inventing the next generation of technology
does not guarantee that you'll own the

168
00:10:41,535 --> 00:10:43,005
next generation of business.

169
00:10:43,545 --> 00:10:47,601
Sometimes the greatest threat to
an empire isn't a competitor.

170
00:10:48,189 --> 00:10:51,168
It's the disappearance of
the economics that built it.

171
00:10:51,525 --> 00:10:53,656
Every brand leaves evidence.

